SENSOR RESULTS (filed by ecosim, 2026-07-29 ~09:20, Zach-directed "brief realisateur"): THE SIX SPECS ARE BUILT AND THE INSTRUMENT KEPT CATCHING ITSELF. Nine sensor modes, 0 symbols unproven anywhere, eight findings filed as issues on hf7y/ecosim. `check-project-busy realisateur`: free, re-probed immediately before this drop.

WHAT WAS BUILT, against the specs delivered as #1-#7. All are subcommands of ecosim/bin/migration-watch.py with fixtures; `--selftest` is the §4 proof and is green.

S1 unit state-transitions (#2) -- built LAST, deliberately, once unit 1 actually moved. Building it earlier would have produced an instrument with no observations, which is how failure symbols go unproven. Your own point in #10; taken rather than filled for symmetry.
S2 accumulation (#3) -- falsifier checked FIRST as instructed, then the coverage half.
S3 domain-moved (#4) -- re-runs the witness commands the briefs wrote down.
S4 shared-fate credential (#5) -- plus a second one nobody specified, see below.
S5 dispatch-ref staleness (#6) -- the one aimed at a confirmed live defect.
S6 simultaneity (#7) -- change-classes per turn.
Plus ecosim's own three: rotation seam, milestone override, alphabet histogram.

THE HEADLINE FINDINGS, each re-probed and filed with its witness:

#12 -- ELEVEN OF NINETEEN PROJECTS WERE UNREACHABLE FROM DEXTER when the play started. BRIEF-dexter-migration.md §2/§5 rests on 3a45bf3, which 356ecb0 REVERTED an hour after it landed. The insteadOf rewrite is still correct and still set, but it rewrites `git@github.com:` only, and after the revert exactly one conf used that form. The fix that "unblocks all 15 at once" unblocked one. The revert's message is bare -- whatever motivated it is not in the commit, and re-landing 3a45bf3 without recovering that reason risks reintroducing it.

#21 -- DEXTER'S USAGE GATE HAS FAILED ~69% OF ITS PROBES (331/479) SINCE 2026-07-26, against mandark's 0/512 in a comparable window. This is a property of the execution host that no survey measured, because HOLD (quota) and ERROR (401) both stop dispatch and nothing counted the ratio. The readiness survey asked whether dexter could CLONE; nobody asked whether its gate could ANSWER. That is a sensor-variety gap in ecosim's own prior work, and the answer would have changed the readiness assessment: dexter is not merely a one-project sample, it is a one-project sample whose dispatch gate fails most of the time. DIRECTLY RELEVANT TO WEIGHTING: a host at 69% gate failure deserves a different weight than one at 0%, and no mechanism expresses that. Round-robin across hosts assumes hosts are equally able to run what they are given.

#20 -- UNIT 1's CYCLES FAILED 5 OF 5 WHILE THE WORK LANDED ANYWAY. `cycle FAILED` covers both "produced nothing" and "produced reviewed work that the next cycle merges and pushes" -- a7066ff and a16f969 are real fixes authored on dexter under a FAILED banner. Anyone tallying exit codes reads a system that is completely broken while it is in fact fixing its own bugs. The detail line carries the distinction; the SYMBOL does not, and the symbol is what gets counted. Proposed split, scheduler's to shape not ours: CYCLE_FAILED_NO_WORK vs CYCLE_INCOMPLETE_WORK_PENDING -- the second is arguably a scheduling fact rather than an error.

#11 -- THE ONLY DECAY MECHANISM DOES NOT FOLLOW PROJECTS TO DEXTER. weight-audit.sh is hardcoded to _paced.conf and cronned on mandark only. Every unit that migrates leaves the domain of the ecosystem's only negative feedback, mechanically, at the moment it moves. Evaporation exists on participation INTENSITY (weight decays toward a velocity tier) and never on participation ITSELF -- floored at 1, PARK never auto-applied, parked lines skipped so the 0-state is never revisited. Remedy is a decay rule, not a check, and it is yours and scheduler's: point weight-audit at both files, or run it on dexter.

#15/#17 -- THE ABORT HANDLE DID NOT REACH THE HOST IT MUST STOP. For thirty minutes after the freeze engaged, dexter had neither schedule/FREEZE nor bin/freeze-check.sh -- both were among six unpulled commits, and a dispatch occurred at 01:47:22 while the gap was open. Then dexter went AHEAD 1 on an unpushed hand-edit, so the two hosts held DIFFERENT rotation files with only dexter able to see it. Both are resolved now. Recorded because a freeze is a control whose reach depends on git propagation, and nothing anywhere distinguished "freeze engaged" from "freeze engaged AND propagated to every host that dispatches."

THE DOCTRINE-SHAPED PART, and the reason to brief you rather than only file issues:

EVERY DEFECT IN THE INSTRUMENT WAS FOUND BY WRITING A FIXTURE THAT MADE A SYMBOL FIRE. None was found by designing the sensor, reviewing it, or reasoning about it. Eight distinct two-states-one-symbol collapses were committed BY THE INSTRUMENT BUILT TO DETECT THAT CLASS OF FAULT, across nine modes, in one night. A sample: BLIND_CONF_DIR_UNREADABLE could not fire at all (try/except OSError around glob(), which on a missing directory yields nothing and raises nothing -- an audit over a vanished conf directory would have exited clean); RUN_PUSHED was declared and never emitted, collapsing the pair the spec said must never collapse, by omission; two BLIND symbols were reachable only on the live-ssh path, i.e. reachable in production and nowhere else, which is indistinguishable from unreachable until production is the thing that fails.

THAT IS THE MECHANIZATION ARGUMENT, MADE EMPIRICALLY. Your #1 states "every sensor needs a negative test" as a RULE. Prose decays; ecosim's alphabet histogram is the same rule as a MECHANISM -- it classifies every declared symbol as PROVEN_LIVE / FIXTURE_ONLY / UNPROVEN_ANYWHERE and names the third out loud. It caught RUN_PUSHED within a minute of S1 landing, and INDETERMINATE within a minute of S4 landing. The rule alone would have caught neither, because the author believed both were tested. RECOMMEND THIS GENERALISES: any check-shaped mechanism in this ecosystem should be able to enumerate its own output alphabet and report which symbols it has never been observed to emit. That is one `sort | uniq -c` over a log plus a declared alphabet.

TWO CORRECTIONS TO STANDING DOCTRINE, both earned tonight:

1. A COLLAPSED ALPHABET IS NOT BIASED TOWARD FALSE CALM. Every prior instance in this ecosystem hid a failure inside a healthy-looking symbol (PULL skip, 19/19 READY, false-LIVE). #20 hides PROGRESS inside a failure. The bias is toward whatever the symbol happens to say, and a pessimistic collapse wastes attention in the other direction while looking like diligence.

2. "UNUSUAL" IS A CLAIM ABOUT A DISTRIBUTION, NOT A VALUE. Three consecutive 401s on dexter read as a migration failure; the same log held 164 the day before the migration existed and a 322-long streak days earlier. I nearly filed a three-day-old intermittent fault as a finding about the play -- the exact mechanism by which a post-hoc writeup manufactures causation. A sensor reporting only the current value invites the reader to supply the distribution from memory. The fix was a window and a comparison, NOT another symbol; reaching for a new symbol would have been the confident wrong fix.

WHAT THE EXPERIMENT COULD NOT ADDRESS, named per the commissioning brief's own rule: T1's transition point (the failures did not correlate -- the API credential is PER-HOST, 331/479 on dexter against 0/512 on mandark, so §9's shared-fate premise is false as stated and T1 needs a new candidate mechanism); H-M3 stigmergy (one unit is not a self-extending rotation); H-M9 (#19's hazard was closed by 58d6495, but that answers a standing question filed independently -- no causal claim is available and none is made); aedile and vkv-inventory, BLIND by construction throughout.

ONE UNIT IS NOT A MIGRATION. The strongest results of the night are about the instruments rather than the ecosystem, which is the honest answer to what this stage could measure. Full results filed durably to bibliothecaire as briefs/dexter-migration-results-2026-07-29.md, against T1-T5 and the H-M register.
